bcc-cc1: hexadecimal floating constants, and a build without atof.

The XENIX bcc-cc1 has no room for atof (a correctly rounding one needs big
integer arithmetic and about 4K of code), so cc has bcc-cpp write every
decimal floating constant in hexadecimal (patch 14), in one fixed form:
0x1.hhhhhhhhhhhhhp+d, or 0x0.hhhhhhhhhhhhhp-1022 for zero and the
denormals. bcc-cc1 now reads that form, building the double's bits
directly; any other hexadecimal floating constant is reported as bad.
Built with -DNO_ATOF (as ports/bcc/build.sh builds the XENIX bcc-cc1), a
decimal floating constant is an error; otherwise it still goes to atof, as
in the host's build. A pp-number now takes a sign after p or P, as after e
or E, for the exponent.

